WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
With a runtime close to three hours&nbsp;
and plenty of jaw-dropping twists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.840
it's easy to miss some crucial&nbsp;
details in "Black Panther:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:12.360
Wakanda Forever." From the "colonizer's" family&nbsp;
to a genius student's backstory, and oh yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.501
an eternal war between two nations, these are&nbsp;
the moments worth taking a closer look at.

00:00:18.037 --> 00:00:19.920
When it was announced that "Wakanda Forever"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.780
would not be recasting the role of King T'Challa,&nbsp;
fans began to speculate about how the sequel would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:29.880
deal with actor Chadwick Boseman's tragic death.&nbsp;
Trailers have made it clear that T'Challa died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.220
in the world of the MCU, but don't reveal how. It&nbsp;
turns out that T'Challa died in much the same way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.960
Boseman himself passed: from an illness he kept&nbsp;
from the public and shared only with his closest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:45.000
loved ones. There's a mural in the film's funeral&nbsp;
scene with Wakandan letters that translate to,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.500
Disney also honored Boseman with a mural.&nbsp;
This similarity is entirely too somber to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.620
call an Easter egg, but it's still&nbsp;
significant and worth discussing.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.300
That T'Challa's death mirrors Boseman's makes&nbsp;
the first part of "Wakanda Forever" even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:05.820
impactful. It also allows director Ryan Coogler,&nbsp;
the cast, and crew to avoid trivializing Boseman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:11.460
fatal illness by substituting his real tragedy&nbsp;
for a possibly cheap fictionalized one. Boseman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.480
family and creative partners, including his&nbsp;
widow Simone, were consultants on the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.101
so the story of T'Challa's death was&nbsp;
handled with their seal of approval.

00:01:19.101 --> 00:01:22.260
"I cried when he called me to, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.440
ask me my thoughts and say is this--&nbsp;
you know, 'How do you feel about this?'"

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.960
That the filmmakers chose not to shoehorn&nbsp;
in an overly complicated end to T'Challa's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.482
story helped "Wakanda Forever" to clear what&nbsp;
was, without a doubt, its highest hurdle.

00:01:35.635 --> 00:01:37.800
One of the many recurring themes that run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.300
throughout "Wakanda Forever" is the conflict&nbsp;
between technology and tradition. Shuri is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.520
squarely on team science and technology, and she&nbsp;
wears them quite literally on her hand. Viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.080
don't have to look that closely to catch the&nbsp;
Princess's new tattoo; it's prominently displayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.580
several times throughout the movie. The design&nbsp;
resembles rearranging particles. Perhaps it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:01.860
visual representation of the chemical makeup of&nbsp;
Vibranium or some other scientific breakthrough.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.540
Shuri rejects the idea of the supernatural&nbsp;
throughout "Wakanda Forever," even after she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.440
experiences it via the ancestral plane&nbsp;
for herself. She tells her mother that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.120
T'Challa didn't touch her shoulder. That was&nbsp;
just a figment of her imagination. She gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.560
up on the heart-shaped herb in favor&nbsp;
of better weapons and suits. Shuri and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.868
M'Baku even reference his accusation&nbsp;
in the first "Black Panther."

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:25.349
"We have watched with disgust as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:30.351
your technological advancements&nbsp;
have been overseen by a child!"

00:02:30.351 --> 00:02:34.860
Many other characters' body modifications&nbsp;
harken back to their heritage. That Shuri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.120
puts her faith so totally in science and her&nbsp;
own genius is a huge part of her identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.100
and her tattoo is a shorthand representation of&nbsp;
that. It's telling that her own Black Panther&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:48.840
ceremony uses the synthetic heart-shaped herb&nbsp;
and takes place without pomp or circumstance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.531
or incense, on an examination&nbsp;
table in a cold laboratory.

00:02:52.935 --> 00:02:54.900
Though Shuri is mostly concerned with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:59.940
technology, much of the rest of "Wakanda Forever"&nbsp;
is focused on the historical and the spiritual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:05.700
especially when it comes to Namor and his undersea&nbsp;
home. Gods are everywhere. Bast, the MCU's Panther&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.680
god loosely based on the Egyptian Bastet, is&nbsp;
the primary deity in Wakanda and is mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.480
multiple times. The Wakandans wear face paint that&nbsp;
looks vaguely panther-like at T'Challa's funeral.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:22.140
M'Baku and the Jabari, however, worship Hanuman, a&nbsp;
gorilla spirit loosely based on a Hindu god of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:27.240
same name. That's why M'Baku wears a gorilla mask&nbsp;
when he challenges T'Challa in "Black Panther" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.980
its why his hand print face paint looks different&nbsp;
from the Wakandans at T'Challa's funeral. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:36.840
are also enormous gorilla statues throughout&nbsp;
Jabariland, including one in M'Baku's throne room.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.760
Mesoamerican iconography is even more prominent&nbsp;
throughout "Wakanda Forever." The room in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:47.460
Namor first speaks with Shuri is covered in murals&nbsp;
of Aztec and Mayan gods, including K'uk'ulkan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.200
which is both one of Namor's aliases and&nbsp;
a mythical creature that the Yucatec Mayan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.520
people really worshiped. M'Baku points out&nbsp;
that Namor's people might consider him a god,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:01.907
based on his nickname. Namor's costume is&nbsp;
also adorned with mesoamerican pictograms.

00:04:02.271 --> 00:04:03.900
It's not entirely a surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:08.640
that Everett Ross is in "Wakanda Forever." After&nbsp;
all, he had a fairly large role in the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.400
in which Shuri used Wakandan technology to save&nbsp;
his life after he was shot. With Shuri promoted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.020
the series protagonist, we were likely to see him&nbsp;
again. How he's used here, though, is thoroughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:24.240
unexpected. "Wakanda Forever" sets the table for&nbsp;
the upcoming "Thunderbolts" movie, thanks to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.700
subplot involving his former marriage to Julia&nbsp;
Louis-Dreyfus's Valentina Allegra de Fontaine.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.180
But for now, the MCU is more concerned&nbsp;
with his relationship with Shuri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.020
to whom he owes an enormous debt.&nbsp;
Upon their first meeting, Shuri says,

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.300
"Don't scare me like that, colonizer!"

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:44.280
…as both a bit of cultural criticism and a cheeky&nbsp;
term of endearment. When she sees Ross again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.460
she greets him with the same pet name&nbsp;
and decides to exchange his debt with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:51.360
her for information about Riri Williams,&nbsp;
who designed the Vibranium detector.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.740
In "Black Panther," Everett becomes&nbsp;
fascinated by the remote Kimoyo beads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.420
that the Wakandans use to stabilize injuries,&nbsp;
including his own, and to communicate. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:05.580
he finds a bracelet of them at the crash site,&nbsp;
which Valentina bugs. When he isn't willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:09.660
help stage an offensive against Wakanda,&nbsp;
he's presumably taken into custody. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:14.460
Okoye and Shuri track Everett down and&nbsp;
free him. Okoye quips now that she's seen a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.540
colonizer in chains, she's seen everything.&nbsp;
Western oppression of indigenous people is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:22.080
a running theme in "Wakanda Forever,"&nbsp;
so this callback works on two levels.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:23.820
Not only does "Wakanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.660
Forever" tee up "Thunderbolts," as well&nbsp;
as introduce a new Black Panther and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.580
new anti-hero in Namor, the sequel also&nbsp;
features the MCU debut of Riri Williams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.840
also known as Ironheart. The character&nbsp;
is getting her own Disney+ series soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:41.040
and fans knew from the trailers that she'd be a&nbsp;
part of "Wakanda Forever," though the film doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.680
use her superhero name yet. Riri's introduction&nbsp;
is refreshingly light on backstory. Most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.600
what we know about her comes via visual clues and&nbsp;
cryptic dialogue as opposed to an exposition dump.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:56.460
In the comics, Riri Williams is a teenage&nbsp;
super genius who hails from Chicago. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.700
fans thought "Wakanda Forever" might&nbsp;
venture to the windy city for Riri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:04.620
but she's already a student at MIT when&nbsp;
Okoye and Shuri go looking for her. She does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:08.580
at least, have a Chicago Bulls pillow&nbsp;
on her bed. Riri also mentions that both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.540
her father and her stepdad have died;&nbsp;
this is the case in the comics as well.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:18.060
The comics version of Riri constructs her first&nbsp;
suit mostly from stolen parts. We likewise see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:22.260
her bartering with another student for parts&nbsp;
in the film, and when Shuri deconstructs Riri's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.520
Vibranium detector, she deduces that it's&nbsp;
assembled from scrap. If you look closely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:30.840
her stud earrings are tiny iron hearts.&nbsp;
Plus, her in-helmet shots call back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:34.660
Tony Stark, as does her mission to fly&nbsp;
dangerously high to stop the drone.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.720
In Marvel comics, Namor rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:41.940
over Atlantis. But Marvel probably wanted to&nbsp;
avoid using the undersea kingdom since DC got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:47.700
to the legendary lost and submerged civilization&nbsp;
first with "Aquaman." Their solution is an elegant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:52.740
and thematically charged improvement on the&nbsp;
existing canon. Filmmakers took Namor McKenzie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:57.360
a half-human half-Atlantian weirdo whose&nbsp;
name is "Roman" spelled backwards and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.900
via a well-researched infusion of&nbsp;
mesoamerican mythology, turned him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:05.640
into 14th-century god. In doing so, they've&nbsp;
effectively transformed the story of Namor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:09.720
into commentary on Spanish conquistadors'&nbsp;
pillaging of Central and South America.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:15.540
Atlantis becomes Talokan, a simplified version of&nbsp;
the name of an Aztec dream world. In mythology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:20.340
it's the realm of Tlālōc the rain god. He gets&nbsp;
a shout-out in the film. It's this entity that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:25.020
Namor's people pray to when they concoct their own&nbsp;
version of the heart-shaped herb potion with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:30.840
own Vibranium-laced plants. In Wakanda, only the&nbsp;
monarch ingests the magic elixir. In the Yucatan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:34.860
everyone does. Namor's mother is hesitant&nbsp;
since she's pregnant, and the mystical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:39.300
substance gives her son strange mutations&nbsp;
like winged feet, pointed ears, long life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:44.580
and the ability to take in oxygen from air&nbsp;
or water. Everyone else turns blue and must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.680
resort to life in the ocean. Namor's mutations&nbsp;
earn him the status of the god K'uk'ulkan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.180
which means feathered serpent god since&nbsp;
he can inhabit the air, land, and sea.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:58.740
"Namor loves his people.&nbsp;
He's going to protect them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:02.807
because to be a ruler, you&nbsp;
have to serve the people."

00:08:02.807 --> 00:08:07.980
It's notable that in the film, a second Vibranium&nbsp;
meteorite hits the Yucatan. The meteor that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:13.239
theorized to have led to the extinction of the&nbsp;
dinosaurs struck there about 65 million years ago.

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:14.700
There's a running joke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:18.840
throughout "Wakanda Forever" that Okoye isn't&nbsp;
too impressed with the latest enhanced outfit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:23.400
that Shuri's been designing for her. The new&nbsp;
armor is much bluer and covers much more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:28.020
the body than what Okoye and the Dora Milaje&nbsp;
are used to wearing. But Shuri insists that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:33.000
the outfit comes with added benefits that will&nbsp;
make it worth her while. Okoye and Aneka don the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:38.220
armor only after Queen Ramonda forces Okoye&nbsp;
to resign. Okoye has to give up her weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:41.880
and Shuri promises her the new&nbsp;
armor comes with a brand new spear.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:47.160
These odd-looking uniforms with their buggy eyes&nbsp;
and tentacle-like appendages are actually comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:51.780
book accurate, both in their appearance and their&nbsp;
symbolic use. In Ta-Nehisi Coates' "Black Panther"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:57.000
run, there's an arc in which Aneka, the Dora&nbsp;
Milaje's combat trainer, kills a chieftain who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:01.380
was mistreating women when it wasn't officially&nbsp;
her place to do so. Though Ayo speaks up on her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:06.180
behalf, Queen Ramonda follows the letter of the&nbsp;
law and sentences her to death. Several members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:10.440
of the all-female fighting force rescue her and&nbsp;
leave the ranks to start their own rebel group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:15.131
the Midnight Angels. This is the name Shuri&nbsp;
gives to the armor in "Wakanda Forever," too.

00:09:15.744 --> 00:09:17.400
Speaking of the Dora Milaje,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:22.020
another storyline from the comics pops up,&nbsp;
however briefly, in "Wakanda Forever." But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:25.860
to fully understand its implications, we have&nbsp;
to go back to an unused scene from "Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:31.380
Panther." The unfinished scene featured a moment&nbsp;
of romantic flirtation between Okoye and Ayo. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:36.660
Okoye married Daniel Kaluuya's W'Kabi. In "Wakanda&nbsp;
Forever," we get another reference to the events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:41.280
of "Black Panther" when Queen Ramonda removes&nbsp;
Okoye from her position and implies that she can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:46.020
still visit W'Kabi in jail, where he is presumably&nbsp;
being held for aligning himself with Killmonger.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:50.100
But near the end of the movie, there's an&nbsp;
acknowledgment of a romantic relationship between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:55.440
Ayo — the new head of the Dora Milaje — and&nbsp;
Aneka. In the Midnight Angels arc in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:00.360
Aneka is the superior member of the Dora Milaje.&nbsp;
It's she who recruits Ayo, but is eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:05.280
bested by her in training. The two butt heads, but&nbsp;
the sexual tension between them is impossible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:10.500
either to ignore. It's Ayo who breaks Aneka out of&nbsp;
prison. The two warrior women dedicate themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:15.300
to each other and to the cause of helping all&nbsp;
those in Wakanda whom the powers that be ignored.

00:10:15.868 --> 00:10:17.040
Ryan Coogler was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:21.000
adamant that Namor the Sub-Mariner would&nbsp;
be the antagonist of "Wakanda Forever."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:25.740
The character is a frequent on-and-off ally&nbsp;
and adversary of Black Panther in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:30.360
and with the change from Atlantis to Talokan,&nbsp;
their secret societies have even more in common.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:36.480
Namor confirms in the film that he is, indeed,&nbsp;
a mutant. In 2012's "Avengers vs. X-Men," Namor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.340
sides with the mutants and floods Wakanda.&nbsp;
T'Challa vows a blood oath to kill Namor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:45.600
which basically locks these two Kings and&nbsp;
these two Kingdoms in an everlasting war.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:51.240
Chadwick Boseman's T'Challa is no longer with&nbsp;
us, so he can't be Namor's foil anymore. Shuri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:54.960
takes up that mantle, too. She gets pretty&nbsp;
friendly pretty quickly with the Feathered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:59.760
Serpent God and tries to find a diplomatic end&nbsp;
to their disagreement. He gifts her his mother's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:03.780
bracelet in return... the very thing that will&nbsp;
help her complete the synthetic heart-shaped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:08.520
herb. But Nakia infiltrates Talokan after&nbsp;
Shuri had convinced Namor she was an ally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:12.600
and she fears the mistake has started&nbsp;
an eternal war. Tensions escalate when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:17.100
Namor attacks Wakanda and kills Queen&nbsp;
Ramonda. Characters, including M'Baku,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:21.300
check her aggression, but Shuri argues that&nbsp;
her mother's death is worth the never-ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:25.440
conflict. "Wakanda Forever" resolves when the&nbsp;
new Black Panther has a chance to get revenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:30.480
and chooses forgiveness and cooperation instead.&nbsp;
Basically, the "Black Panther" sequel trades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:35.641
T'Challa for Shuri and runs through the comics'&nbsp;
eternal war in about two hours and 40 minutes.

